Memory Leak: Memory usage grows continuously, never released. Leads to OutOfMemoryError.

**Symptoms**
- Heap usage continuously increasing
- Frequent Full GC cycles
- Eventually: `java.lang.OutOfMemoryError: Java heap space`
- Application becomes unresponsive

**Step 1: Identify Memory Growth**

```bash
# Monitor heap usage
jstat -gc <pid> 1000
# Output columns:
# S0C, S1C: Survivor space capacity
# S0U, S1U: Survivor space used
# EC, EU: Eden space capacity/used
# OC, OU: Old generation capacity/used
# MC, MU: Metaspace capacity/used

# Watch Old Generation (OU) - should stabilize after GC
# If OU keeps growing after Full GC = memory leak

# Or use JVM flags for GC logging
-Xlog:gc*:file=/var/log/gc.log:time,uptime,level,tags

# Analyze GC logs
# Look for: Old Gen usage after Full GC keeps increasing
```

**Step 2: Capture Heap Dump**

```bash
# Option 1: Manual heap dump
jmap -dump:live,format=b,file=/tmp/heap-dump.hprof <pid>

# Option 2: Automatic on OOM
# Add JVM flags:
-XX:+HeapDumpOnOutOfMemoryError
-XX:HeapDumpPath=/var/log/heap-dumps

# Option 3: Via JMX (production-safe)
jcmd <pid> GC.heap_dump /tmp/heap-dump.hprof

# Heap dump will be large (same size as heap)
# 4GB heap = 4GB dump file
```

**Step 3: Analyze Heap Dump**

```bash
# Option 1: Eclipse MAT (Memory Analyzer Tool)
# Download from: https://www.eclipse.org/mat/
./mat.sh /tmp/heap-dump.hprof

# MAT shows:
# 1. Leak Suspects Report (automatic)
# 2. Dominator Tree (largest objects)
# 3. Histogram (object counts by class)

# Option 2: Command-line analysis
jhat /tmp/heap-dump.hprof
# Opens web interface at http://localhost:7000

# Option 3: VisualVM
jvisualvm
# File → Load → select heap dump
```

**Step 4: Common Leak Patterns**

```java
// Pattern 1: Static Collections Growing Unbounded
public class CacheManager {
  private static Map<String, User> userCache = new HashMap<>();

  public static void cacheUser(User user) {
    userCache.put(user.getId(), user); // Never removed!
  }
}

// Fix: Use bounded cache
import com.google.common.cache.Cache;
import com.google.common.cache.CacheBuilder;

public class CacheManager {
  private static Cache<String, User> userCache = CacheBuilder.newBuilder()
    .maximumSize(10000)           // Max 10K entries
    .expireAfterWrite(1, TimeUnit.HOURS)  // Auto-expire
    .build();

  public static void cacheUser(User user) {
    userCache.put(user.getId(), user);
  }
}

// Pattern 2: Unclosed Resources
public class OrderService {
  public void processOrders() {
    InputStream is = new FileInputStream("orders.txt"); // Never closed!
    // Process file
  }
}

// Fix: Use try-with-resources
public class OrderService {
  public void processOrders() {
    try (InputStream is = new FileInputStream("orders.txt")) {
      // Process file
    } catch (IOException e) {
      log.error("Error processing orders", e);
    }
  }
}

// Pattern 3: Listeners Not Unregistered
public class EventProcessor {
  private EventBus eventBus;

  public void start() {
    eventBus.register(this); // Registered but never unregistered
  }

  @Subscribe
  public void handleEvent(OrderEvent event) {
    // Process event
  }
}

// Fix: Unregister when done
public class EventProcessor {
  private EventBus eventBus;

  public void start() {
    eventBus.register(this);
  }

  @PreDestroy
  public void stop() {
    eventBus.unregister(this); // Clean up
  }
}

// Pattern 4: ThreadLocal Not Cleared
public class RequestContext {
  private static ThreadLocal<User> currentUser = new ThreadLocal<>();

  public static void setCurrentUser(User user) {
    currentUser.set(user); // Never removed!
  }
}

// Fix: Clear ThreadLocal
public class RequestContext {
  private static ThreadLocal<User> currentUser = new ThreadLocal<>();

  public static void setCurrentUser(User user) {
    currentUser.set(user);
  }

  public static void clear() {
    currentUser.remove(); // Clean up thread-local
  }
}

// Use in filter
@Component
public class RequestContextFilter extends OncePerRequestFilter {
  @Override
  protected void doFilterInternal(HttpServletRequest request, 
                                   HttpServletResponse response, 
                                   FilterChain filterChain) {
    try {
      User user = extractUser(request);
      RequestContext.setCurrentUser(user);
      filterChain.doFilter(request, response);
    } finally {
      RequestContext.clear(); // Always clear
    }
  }
}
```

**Step 5: Memory Leak Detection Tool**

```java
// Enable NMT (Native Memory Tracking)
// JVM flags:
-XX:NativeMemoryTracking=detail

// Check memory usage
jcmd <pid> VM.native_memory summary

// Compare snapshots
jcmd <pid> VM.native_memory baseline
# Wait some time
jcmd <pid> VM.native_memory summary.diff

// Shows memory allocation by category:
// - Java Heap
// - Thread stacks
// - Code cache
// - GC
// - Metaspace
```

**Prevention**

```java
// 1. Monitor heap usage
@Component
public class MemoryMonitor {
  @Scheduled(fixedDelay = 60000) // Every minute
  public void checkMemory() {
    MemoryMXBean memoryBean = ManagementFactory.getMemoryMXBean();
    MemoryUsage heapUsage = memoryBean.getHeapMemoryUsage();

    long used = heapUsage.getUsed();
    long max = heapUsage.getMax();
    double percentage = (double) used / max * 100;

    if (percentage > 90) {
      log.warn("High heap usage: {}%", percentage);
      // Alert or trigger heap dump
    }
  }
}

// 2. Use weak references for caches
import java.lang.ref.WeakReference;

public class SessionManager {
  private Map<String, WeakReference<Session>> sessions = new HashMap<>();

  public void addSession(String id, Session session) {
    sessions.put(id, new WeakReference<>(session));
    // Session can be GC'd if not used
  }
}

// 3. Regular memory profiling
// Use VisualVM, YourKit, JProfiler in staging

// 4. Bounded collections
import org.apache.commons.collections4.map.LRUMap;

public class RecentOrdersCache {
  private Map<String, Order> cache = new LRUMap<>(1000); // Max 1000
}
```

Key steps: Monitor → Detect growth → Heap dump → Analyze → Find pattern → Fix → Verify.

Hanging Application: Application stops responding, threads blocked, no progress. Different from crash (process exits) or slow (makes progress slowly).

**Step 1: Confirm Application is Hanging**

```bash
# Check if process is running
ps aux | grep java

# Check CPU usage
top -p <pid>
# If CPU 0% = likely deadlock/blocked
# If CPU 100% = infinite loop

# Check if responding to requests
curl -v --max-time 5 http://localhost:8080/actuator/health
# Timeout = hanging

# Check thread count
jcmd <pid> Thread.print | grep "java.lang.Thread.State" | sort | uniq -c
# High WAITING/BLOCKED count = likely deadlock
```

**Step 2: Capture Thread Dump**

```bash
# Option 1: jstack (most common)
jstack -l <pid> > thread-dump.txt

# Option 2: jcmd
jcmd <pid> Thread.print > thread-dump.txt

# Option 3: kill -3 (sends SIGQUIT)
kill -3 <pid>
# Output goes to stdout/stderr (check logs)

# Option 4: Via JMX
jconsole → Threads tab → Detect Deadlock

# Take 3-5 thread dumps, 10 seconds apart
for i in {1..5}; do
  jstack -l <pid> > thread-dump-$i.txt
  sleep 10
done
# Compare to see which threads never change (stuck)
```

**Step 3: Analyze Thread Dump**

```java
